{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001135","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"For target trait “arthritis” (broad), PGS001135 is the most direct endpoint match in the fixed list: trait_efo is exactly “arthritis” and phenotyping_reported is “Arthritis (nos)”. Per the trait-label guidance, phenotyping_reported is the primary endpoint-fidelity field, so this direct generic-arthritis endpoint is preferred over narrower arthritis subtypes (e.g., juvenile idiopathic arthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, spondyloarthropathy, gout, osteoarthritis) when the target is unspecified.

Performance-wise, PGS001135 reports PRS-comparable metrics (explicit PGS-only AUROC 0.539 and PGS R2 (no covariates) 0.00291) plus incremental AUROC (0.00314) and full-model AUROC (0.674) with basic covariates (age, sex, array type, PCs). The performance-metrics guidance emphasizes that PRS-only / covariates-regressed-out metrics are the most reliable cross-model indicators, and that full-model AUROC should not be treated as standalone PRS performance—so PGS001135’s explicit PGS-only reporting is a quality/interpretability advantage versus many candidates that only show full-model AUROC (often with unknown or non-comparable covariates).

Heritability sanity-check for “arthritis” shows low SNP h2 for broad/unspecified arthritis phenotypes in UKB-style definitions (e.g., self-reported arthritis (nos) h2 ~0.0006–0.0045; ICD10 M13 “Other arthritis” h2 ~0.0024–0.0073), which is consistent with PGS001135’s modest PRS-only R2 and supports that its limited discrimination is plausible for this broad, heterogeneous endpoint rather than indicating a reporting artifact."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001340","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS Model Evaluator framework (skill_overview + performance_metrics): among direct-match atrial fibrillation candidates, prioritize endpoint fidelity plus PRS-comparable performance (PGS-only metrics) over covariate-inflated full-model discrimination. PGS001340 is a direct AF endpoint (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all AF) and uniquely provides explicit PRS-only discrimination/variance metrics in Europeans (PGS AUROC no covariates ≈0.613; PGS R2 no covariates ≈0.023; plus incremental AUROC reported), with a reasonably large European validation sample (n=24,905). Many other candidates report only full-model AUROC/C-index (often with substantial clinical covariates such as CHARGE-AF) or only effect sizes, which the skill notes are less comparable for cross-model ranking when PRS-only metrics exist.

Heritability sanity-check (get_heritability_records, EUR): AF observed h2 estimates are modest (~0.02–0.03 in large GWAS-atlas/UKB-derived records; liability-scale values vary by phenotype definition). PGS001340’s PRS-only R2 (~0.023) is plausible relative to these h2 records (i.e., not obviously exceeding a reasonable genetic-variance ceiling), supporting interpretability of the reported PRS-only metric.

Therefore, PGS001340 is the best-supported high-quality direct AF PGS in this fixed candidate list given the availability of clean PRS-only performance evidence and strong endpoint alignment."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001263","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint fidelity (primary): Per trait-label guidance, phenotyping_reported is the primary endpoint-fidelity field. PGS001263 is the only candidate with phenotyping_reported="Atrial flutter" (and trait_efo="atrial flutter"), making it the cleanest direct match to the target. The other candidates are broader composites (atrial fibrillation + flutter) and one is explicitly time-to-event, which is a mild endpoint mismatch for a generic disease target.

Performance interpretation (PRS-comparable): Per performance-metrics guidance, PRS-only metrics (e.g., "PGS AUROC (no covariates)", "PGS R2 (no covariates)") are the most reliable cross-model signals; full-model AUROC is often covariate-driven. PGS001263 reports PRS-only AUROC 0.617 and PRS-only R² 0.025 in European validation (n=24,905), plus incremental AUROC 0.0217 over basic covariates (age, sex, PCs, array). This is slightly stronger than the closest composite alternative PGS001339 (PRS-only AUROC 0.613; PRS-only R² 0.024), and far more directly comparable than PGS001841/PGS002050 which report only partial correlation (no AUROC/R² here).

Heritability sanity-check: Local EUR h2 records matching the closest available phenotype (ICD10 I48 atrial fibrillation and flutter) show observed-scale h2 ~0.015–0.021 (liability h2 ~0.146–0.204). A PRS-only R² ~0.025 for a closely related flutter endpoint is plausible in magnitude and not obviously inconsistent with these h2 ceilings (noting scale/phenotype differences), supporting that PGS001263’s PRS-only performance is credible and not merely covariate inflation."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000448","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS Model Evaluator framework (skill_overview: compare endpoint fidelity, performance evidence type, method/structure, covariate cleanliness, and study context), all candidates are direct matches for basal cell carcinoma by trait_reported/trait_efo, so endpoint fidelity does not separate them. Per the performance-metrics guidance (skill section performance_metrics), none of the candidates provide PRS-only AUROC/R2; therefore full-model AUROC differences are not a reliable cross-model ranking axis, and effect sizes (OR per SD; top-percentile OR) become important supportive evidence when covariates are broadly comparable.

Within the large PRSweb/Cancer PRSweb 2020-12-15 sibling set (PGS000445–PGS000459) evaluated in Europeans with basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4), PGS000448 (lassosum; 183 variants) has the strongest per-SD effect size (OR 1.411, 95% CI 1.335–1.491) and the strongest top-1% vs rest enrichment (OR 2.93, 95% CI 2.03–4.25), while maintaining a similar full-model AUROC (0.587) to nearby siblings—making it the best-supported candidate in this fixed list. Alternatives like PGS000452/PGS000454 have slightly higher full-model AUROC (~0.632) but, given the lack of PRS-only metrics, that advantage is less decisive than the stronger effect-size evidence for PGS000448 under comparable covariate adjustment.

Heritability sanity-check (get_heritability_records): local EUR LDSC records for self-reported basal cell carcinoma show low observed-scale h2 (~0.010–0.013) but materially higher liability-scale h2 (~0.14–0.21), consistent with a complex disease where moderate PRS effect sizes (as seen here) are plausible and where modest full-model AUROCs should not be over-interpreted as PRS-only performance."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002786","confidence":"Low","rationale":"PGS002786 is the closest semantic match to the target trait (trait_reported/trait_efo are both “Bipolar disorder”), whereas PGS002787 and PGS002788 are bipolar I and bipolar II subtypes rather than the umbrella disorder. However, endpoint fidelity is weak across all three candidates: phenotyping_reported reflects cognition/behavior measures rather than a direct bipolar disorder diagnosis endpoint, which is a warning signal for using these scores as generic bipolar disorder risk predictors (read_skill_section('trait_labels')).

On performance interpretation, none of the candidates report PRS-only AUC/R²; the only metric shown is a small partial R² in a model adjusted for basic covariates (age, PCs1-3). Per the performance-metrics guidance, such full/partial-model metrics are less directly comparable to standalone PRS performance, and small differences between similar candidates are not decisive; still, PGS002786 has comparable (slightly lower) partial R² than the subtype scores and does not compensate for the subtype mismatch those introduce (read_skill_section('performance_metrics')).

Trait heritability records for bipolar disorder in EUR show substantial SNP heritability on the liability scale in GWAS-Atlas entries (~0.17–0.27), indicating meaningful genetic signal exists in principle; the very small reported partial R² here is therefore more consistent with the non-diagnostic evaluation phenotype and/or metric type than with a strong disease-risk PRS (get_heritability_records('bipolar disorder')). Given the fixed list, PGS002786 is the best available direct match but remains sub-optimal due to phenotype mismatch and limited PRS-comparable performance evidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004621","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"For the target trait cirrhosis of liver, the most direct endpoint matches in the candidate list are those with trait_efo = "cirrhosis of liver" (PGS000726, PGS000776, PGS004621). Using the PRS-evaluator guidance on performance interpretation (skill section: performance_metrics), none of these candidates provides a clearly PRS-only AUROC/R2; the reported AUROC/C-index values are full-model style metrics with basic covariates, so small differences (e.g., 0.677 vs 0.68) are not a strong differentiator. In that situation, the skill recommends leaning more on structural signals like method properties and variant coverage rather than over-weighting small full-model metric gaps.

PGS004621 is therefore preferred because it is a genome-wide PRS-CS score with very broad variant coverage (1,089,806 variants) and a very large European evaluation sample (n=1,153,995) with only basic covariates (age, sex, 10 PCs), which reduces concerns about covariate-driven inflation (performance_metrics section). Publication context (skill section: publication_context) is treated as a weak signal, but here it supports that PGS004621 comes from a disease-focused cirrhosis genetics paper (Nat Genet 2024) rather than a generic pan-trait framework.

Trait heritability context from local EUR LDSC records for cirrhosis-related endpoints is low on the observed scale (e.g., h2 ~0.0007–0.0083 for ICD10 K74 fibrosis/cirrhosis and related self-report items). This makes modest discrimination metrics plausible and reinforces that we should not expect very high PRS-only performance; it does not contradict selecting the best-validated, genome-wide direct-endpoint model among the candidates."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004911","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Trait/endpoint alignment (prs_model_evaluator trait-label guidance): PGS004911 has coherent direct-disease labeling across trait_reported/trait_efo and, critically, phenotyping_reported = hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. By contrast, PGS000778’s phenotyping_reported is restricted to “clinical events in individuals with a pathogenic or likely pathogenic sarcomeric variant,” which is a special-population endpoint and weaker for general HCM risk deployment. The “(MTAG)” label on PGS004911 is not an endpoint mismatch and is mildly preferred within a publication family because it can boost power while still targeting the same disease.

Performance/covariate comparability (prs_model_evaluator performance-metrics guidance): none of the candidates provide clearly labeled PGS-only AUROC/R², so full-model metrics must be interpreted cautiously and covariate packaging matters. PGS000739 reports a high AUROC (0.821) but with heavy clinical covariates and rare-variant carrier status, making that AUROC less informative about the standalone PGS. PGS004911 is evaluated with basic covariates (age, age^2, sex, PCs) and, within the same 2025 Nat Genet study family and same EUR validation setting (n=343,182), it shows stronger reported discrimination/variance explained than its sibling PGS004910 (AUROC 0.80 vs 0.73; R² 0.048 vs 0.031), supporting PGS004911 as the best candidate in this fixed list.

Heritability sanity-check (local h2 lookup): get_heritability_records for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy returned closely related EUR ‘Cardiomyopathy’ records with low observed-scale h2 (~0.002–0.006) but substantially higher liability-scale h2 (~0.17–0.69), consistent with a low-prevalence disease where modest reported R² values like 0.048 are plausible and not obviously inconsistent with a heritability ceiling. Overall, PGS004911 is the strongest direct HCM PGS here given endpoint fidelity, modern genome-wide PRS-CS construction (~374k variants), large EUR validation, and the best within-family performance under clean covariates."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002767","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS-evaluator guidance on performance interpretation and publication context: pan-trait UKB framework scores (e.g., the PLoS Genet “across 813 traits” paper) are a known weak signal for per-disease deployment compared with disease-focused alternatives, even when they report explicit PRS-only metrics (publication-context section). Here, PGS001192 is exactly such a pan-trait UKB framework score and its PRS-only performance is modest (PGS-only AUROC 0.5566; PGS-only R² 0.0068), with most of the full-model AUROC (0.645) coming from covariates (incremental AUROC 0.0104), consistent with the performance-metrics guidance.

Among the remaining direct knee osteoarthritis candidates, PGS002767 (PRS-CS; 1,052,275 variants; EUR evaluation; n=39,444) provides the strongest standalone PRS signal via effect size (OR per SD 1.35, CI 1.30–1.40), which the performance-metrics section treats as meaningful discrimination evidence when AUC/R² are absent and as a reasonable tie-breaker versus candidates with only full-model AUROC or weaker/less-informative evidence. PGS002729 reports only a covariate-including full-model AUROC (0.66 with age/sex/BMI) and no PRS-only metrics, making it hard to compare cleanly; PGS002749 has very small validation (n=1,422) and only OR 1.13.

Trait heritability (EUR) for knee OA from local records is moderate (e.g., h2≈0.21 for “Osteoarthritis of knee”), so modest PRS performance is expected; this h2 context supports not over-weighting covariate-boosted full-model AUROC and instead preferring the candidate with the clearest PRS-specific signal among disease-aligned options."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004606","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS Model Evaluator overview framework (compare endpoint fidelity, comparable performance evidence, transportability context, polygenic breadth/method, covariate cleanliness, and publication/validation context), the strongest direct-match candidate for the target trait “macular degeneration” is the AMD-specific PRS-CS score PGS004606: it has clean endpoint alignment (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all AMD), modern genome-wide method/coverage (~1,000,946 variants), and large European evaluation (n=163,011) with only basic covariates (age, sex, PCs). Although it reports only full-model AUROC (0.71) rather than explicit PRS-only AUC/R2, it also reports a strong per-SD effect size (OR=1.76), which is supportive evidence of meaningful PRS discrimination.

By contrast, the UKB high-throughput framework score PGS001013 provides explicit PRS-only metrics but they are weak (PGS-only AUROC≈0.553; PGS-only R2≈0.006; incremental AUROC≈0.006), suggesting limited standalone PRS signal despite a higher covariate-boosted full-model AUROC. The portability-study siblings (PGS001834/PGS002041) provide only small partial correlations (~0.016–0.018) rather than standard discrimination metrics. PGS004607 is trained on an AFR GWAS and evaluated in EUR with lower AUROC (0.65), making it less suitable for a EUR-targeted pick here. PGS004952 reports a very high AUROC but has a very small validation sample (n=1,232) and a suspiciously formatted AUROC value (84.2), reducing reliability.

Trait heritability sanity-check: local EUR h2 records for macular degeneration/AMD show non-trivial SNP heritability (e.g., Age-related Macular Degeneration h2≈0.067–0.190; UKB ‘Eye problems/disorders: Macular degeneration’ h2≈0.010), consistent with expecting a PRS to have meaningful but not extreme standalone performance; PGS004606’s strong OR and moderate AUROC are plausible in that context."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001255","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS model evaluator framework (skill_overview + performance-metrics guidance): both candidates are direct matches to the target trait (trait_efo=otosclerosis) and are UK Biobank-derived, so the comparison hinges on endpoint fidelity plus the most comparable performance evidence, covariate cleanliness, and validation context.

PGS001255 is preferred because it reports explicit PRS-comparable validation metrics in Europeans (PGS-only AUROC=0.6319; PGS-only R²=0.01341) alongside full-model metrics with only basic covariates (age, sex, array type, PCs). This makes its predictive evidence directly interpretable as standalone PRS performance. PGS002046, while larger in variant count and training N, does not provide AUROC/R² here and only reports partial correlation (partial-r=0.0182), which is less directly comparable as a discrimination metric for choosing a single best PGS.

Heritability sanity-check (EUR LDSC local records for otosclerosis-related UKB endpoints): observed-scale h2 estimates are low (~0.0023–0.0076; liability-scale values are much higher but prevalence-dependent). PGS001255’s PRS-only R² (~0.013) is not obviously impossible given these noisy/endpoint-dependent h2 records and supports a modest but non-trivial PRS signal; it also avoids relying on covariate-boosted full-model AUROC alone. Overall, PGS001255 has the clearest PRS-only performance evidence for otosclerosis in the provided candidate set."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000083","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS Model Evaluator overview framework (endpoint fidelity, comparable performance evidence, covariate cleanliness/packaging, validation breadth, and method/structure), the strongest direct-match candidate in this fixed list is PGS000083. It aligns tightly to the target (“pancreatic carcinoma”) via trait_reported pancreatic cancer, trait_efo exocrine pancreatic carcinoma, and an incident pancreatic cancer phenotype. Its evaluation is in Europeans with a very large validation sample (n=391,491) and shows moderate-to-strong PRS signal via HR=1.49 per SD; the reported AUROC=0.745 is a full-model metric with many covariates, so it is not treated as PRS-only discrimination, but it remains supportive within a clean, direct endpoint.

Competing candidates are weaker on the same framework: PGS000385/PGS000386/PGS004693 have substantially lower full-model AUROCs (~0.54–0.59) in UKB-style evaluations; PGS000725 has lower AUROC (0.639). PGS002264 reports a much higher AUROC (0.83) but is heavily clinically packaged (diabetes status/onset, waist circumference, family history/clinical risk), which the performance-metrics guidance flags as making AUROC less comparable to standalone PRS quality; additionally its endpoint is pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (narrower than the target’s broader carcinoma framing) and validation ancestry is not reported.

Heritability sanity-check (EUR): local records for pancreatic cancer show modest SNP-heritability (h2≈0.0568; liability h2≈0.0893), consistent with expecting only moderate standalone PRS performance and reinforcing caution about over-interpreting covariate-boosted AUROCs. Given these constraints, PGS000083 is the most defensible high-quality direct-match choice from the provided candidates."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004163","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint fidelity (trait labels): The target is rheumatoid arthritis. PGS004163 has trait_efo=rheumatoid arthritis and phenotyping_reported=Seropositive RA, which is a coherent RA diagnosis endpoint (trait-label guidance: phenotyping_reported is primary; subtype endpoints can still be strong matches for a generic RA target when coherent and well-supported). Several other candidates are non-RA inflammatory arthritides (juvenile idiopathic arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis) and are poorer matches.

Performance-metric interpretation (performance_metrics guidance): For cross-model ranking, PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2) are preferred; full-model AUROC can be covariate-driven and is not a substitute unless covariates are minimal/clean. In the 2023 Am J Hum Genet method-comparison family, PGS004163 reports strong discrimination and variance explained (full_model_auc≈0.747; R2≈0.138) with covariates listed as 0, which is a positive comparability signal (no covariate packaging/leakage). It also has a strong per-SD effect size (OR≈2.46), which the performance-metrics guidance treats as meaningful evidence of PRS discriminative power when AUC/R2 comparability is otherwise acceptable. Competing RA models in the same family (e.g., PGS004049 LDpred2.CV) are slightly weaker on both AUC and R2, while older UKB snpnet RA models (PGS001310/PGS001309) have much lower AUC (~0.665) and PGS-only R2 (~0.015).

Heritability sanity-check (h2): Local EUR heritability records for rheumatoid arthritis show non-trivial SNP-heritability in external GWAS resources (e.g., GWAS Atlas RA h2 ~0.13–0.17). This supports that an RA PRS can explain meaningful variance; the chosen model’s reported R2 is directionally plausible relative to this ceiling (used as a sanity check, not a ranking formula)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000136","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS Model Evaluator framework (skill_overview + performance_metrics): first prioritize endpoint fidelity and comparability. PGS000133–PGS000136 are all direct matches for schizophrenia (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all schizophrenia), share the same publication family and method (PRS-CS), and use only basic covariates (age, sex, PCs, platform/batch), so they are comparable within this sibling set. None report PRS-only AUC/R2, so full-model AUROC is only supportive (not a primary cross-study ranking metric), and effect sizes (OR per SD) become useful tie-breakers when present.

Within this set, PGS000136 has better supportive performance than the two lowest siblings (full-model AUROC 0.64 vs 0.60 for PGS000133/PGS000134) and also reports a strong OR per SD (~1.7), consistent with meaningful PRS discrimination. PGS000135 shows a higher AUROC (0.74) but comes from a much smaller validation sample (n=9,569) and provides no effect-size signal, making that AUROC more likely to be an unstable outlier rather than a reliably superior PRS.

Heritability sanity check (get_heritability_records for schizophrenia): EUR LDSC records show substantial SNP-heritability on the liability scale (multiple entries around h2_liability ~0.24–0.31), which supports expecting a meaningful but not implausibly extreme PRS signal; this backdrop further argues against over-weighting a single small-N AUROC spike and favors the more stable, effect-size-supported sibling (PGS000136)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000471","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS Model Evaluator overview framework (compare endpoint fidelity, comparable performance evidence, method/structure, covariate cleanliness, and within-publication sibling comparison), all three candidates are direct matches to the target trait (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all align to skin carcinoma in situ) and come from the same publication family with the same EUR-only GWAS/DEV/EVAL context, similar training size (n=6,005), and the same light covariate set (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4). None report PRS-only AUC/R²; the available AUROC/R² are full-model metrics, so they are supportive mainly for within-family comparison rather than as fully PRS-comparable metrics (per the performance-metrics guidance).

Within this like-for-like set, PGS000471 (lassosum) has the strongest validation signals: highest full-model AUROC (0.569 vs 0.557 vs 0.524), highest Nagelkerke pseudo-R² (0.0255 vs 0.0154 vs 0.00141), and strongest effect sizes (OR per SD 1.401; top 1% vs rest OR 3.77), making it the best-supported candidate.

Trait-specific heritability lookup for closely matching EUR records (UKBB LDSC for ICD10 D04 carcinoma in situ of skin) shows low observed-scale h2 estimates (~0.0007–0.0041; liability-scale values higher but with low z-scores), which is consistent with modest predictive performance and does not suggest the reported metrics are implausibly inflated. Therefore, PGS000471 is recommended as the best available PGS among the fixed candidates."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003213","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS Model Evaluator overview framework (compare endpoint fidelity, comparable performance evidence, covariate cleanliness, method/structure, and study context), the candidates split into: (a) ExPRSweb sleep-apnea scores (PGS003204–PGS003218) with basic covariates and very modest full-model AUROC (~0.49–0.53) and (b) obstructive-sleep-apnea scores with much higher full-model AUROC (~0.77–0.79) but heavier covariates including BMI and multi-ancestry packaging, making those AUROCs less comparable to standalone PRS performance per the performance-metrics guidance.

Within the direct sleep-apnea ExPRSweb set (cleaner covariates: age/sex/PCs/batch/array), PGS003213 (PRS-CS; UKB-trained) has the highest reported AUROC (0.527, 95% CI 0.517–0.536) and the strongest per-SD effect size (OR 1.106, 95% CI 1.071–1.142), so it is the best-supported choice among the fixed candidates despite overall weak discrimination.

Trait heritability lookups for sleep apnoea in EUR are low on the observed scale (h2 ~0.003–0.006; liability-scale h2 ~0.067–0.146), which is consistent with expecting only modest PRS performance and supports labeling the recommendation as direct but sub-optimal rather than high-quality."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000461","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Trait/endpoint match: PGS000461 is a direct match to the target (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all “Squamous cell carcinoma”). Several other candidates are different cancers (esophageal) or different SCC sites (head/neck, lung) or a restricted survivorship endpoint (“Risk of squamous cell carcinoma in childhood cancer survivors”), which trait-label guidance flags as weaker for generic SCC risk.

Performance interpretation (per performance-metrics guidance): none of the SCC candidates report explicit PGS-only AUROC/R², so we should not treat full-model AUROC as a pure PRS metric; instead, compare within the direct-match set where covariates are broadly similar/basic (age, sex, batch, PCs). Within that comparable cluster, PGS000461 has the strongest overall performance package: full-model AUROC 0.599 and the highest reported Nagelkerke pseudo-R² (0.0304), plus a relatively strong per-SD effect size (OR 1.49) and strong tail separation (top 1% vs rest OR 3.51). Alternatives are slightly weaker on these same fields (e.g., PGS000463 AUROC 0.593, R² 0.0268; PGS000465 AUROC 0.589, R² 0.0282; UKB-trained PGS000462/464/466/468 AUROC ~0.57–0.575 with R² ~0.014–0.015). PGS000731 has slightly higher AUROC (0.605) but has unknown covariates and no training N reported, lowering comparability/cleanliness.

Heritability sanity-check: local EUR h2 records for self-reported squamous cell carcinoma show low observed-scale h2 (~0.0017–0.0032) but higher liability-scale h2 (~0.13–0.23). This supports expecting modest PRS performance and does not contradict selecting the best-supported direct SCC model in this candidate set."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All candidates are direct-label matches to urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported bladder cancer; trait_efo urinary bladder carcinoma/cancer; phenotyping reported bladder cancer/incident bladder cancer/cancer of bladder), so selection hinges on comparative evidence quality per the PRS Model Evaluator procedure: endpoint fidelity, comparable performance evidence (prefer PRS-comparable signals over covariate-boosted full-model AUROC), covariate cleanliness, and study/validation context (read_skill_section: skill_overview; performance_metrics).

Several models (PGS000071, PGS000782) report very high full-model AUROC (~0.80) but include substantial non-genetic risk factors (smoking variables, BMI, etc.), making those AUROCs poor proxies for standalone PRS quality per the performance-metrics guidance. In contrast, the PRSweb family (PGS000607–PGS000616) uses basic covariates (age/sex/batch/PCs) and provides more PRS-informative signals (Nagelkerke R² and tail ORs). Within that family, PGS000611 (GWAS Hits, 13 variants; UKB-based; eval n=13,530) has among the strongest combined evidence: AUROC 0.567 with Nagelkerke R² 0.0114 and a strong tail separation (OR top 1% vs rest 2.66, CI 1.79–3.93), while other PRSweb bladder scores show lower AUROC/R² and/or weaker/unstable tail ORs.

Trait heritability lookup for “urinary bladder cancer” in local EUR records shows very low observed h2 for self-reported bladder cancer (h2 ~0.0007–0.0011; liability h2 ~0.048–0.054), which supports cautious expectations for PRS-explained variance and reinforces that extremely high full-model AUROC values are likely driven by covariates rather than genetic score signal (get_heritability_records)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Trait/endpoint alignment: For a uterine cancer target, endometrial carcinoma is the clinically dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma and is therefore an acceptable direct match when better supported than umbrella uterine labels (prs_model_evaluator skill: trait_labels). PGS003381 targets “uterine endometrial carcinoma” with matching phenotyping_reported.

Performance interpretation: Cross-model ranking should not rely on full-model AUROC alone unless PRS-only metrics are available and covariates are comparable; PRS-only R²/AUROC (or covariates-regressed-out metrics) are the most informative when present (prs_model_evaluator skill: performance_metrics). In this list, the exact-label uterine cancer model PGS001795 reports a PRS-comparable metric (Nagelkerke’s R² with covariates regressed out = 0.001948) that is very small, suggesting weak standalone PRS signal despite very large variant count. The umbrella uterine cancer PRSweb models (PGS000541/PGS000542) show low discrimination (AUROC ~0.57; pseudo-R² ~0.013) with basic covariates. PGS003381, while not explicitly labeled as PGS-only, combines strong endpoint match with a genome-wide method (lassosum; 529,365 variants) and reports higher discrimination (full-model AUROC 0.761; R² 0.11) using relatively light/basic covariates (age + PCs), making its reported performance more interpretable than models whose apparent performance could be heavily covariate-packaged.

Heritability sanity check: Local EUR h2 records for related self-reported “uterine/endometrial cancer” show low observed h2 but non-zero liability-scale h2 (~0.0116), consistent with expecting modest PRS-only R² and cautioning against over-interpreting covariate-boosted full-model metrics (get_heritability_records: uterine cancer). Given the fixed candidates, PGS003381 offers the best combination of phenotype fidelity to the dominant uterine cancer subtype and a modern genome-wide PRS construction with comparatively strong reported discrimination."}
